  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain returns Thursday, with a transition to\nupslope snow showers for the mountains Friday. Colder and \nwindy conditions develop and last through the weekend.\n\nAs a closed low takes a southern then easterly trajectory into\nthe Mid Atlantic towards the weekend, reinforcing cold air \nwaves will push temperatures down into the 30s and 40s for highs\nFriday and Saturday for the mountains, while the Piedmont drops\ninto the 50s for highs. Looking at NAEFS temperature anomalies,\nbeginning Thursday afternoon through next Wednesday, 850mb \ntemperatures will be 1 to 2 standard deviations below normal in \nthe area. Overnight lows will fall into the 20s and 30s for lows\nby Thursday night in the mountains, with strong cold air \nadvecting into the Piedmont by Friday night into early next \nweek. There will be several nights of hard killing freezes \nbeginning Friday morning, with even the Piedmont dropping into \nthe upper 20s to low 30s. \n\nThe other concern will be strong and gusty westerly and then NW \nwinds which will begin Thursday and continue into the weekend. \nGusts between 30 to 40 mph will be possible for the mountain \nridges and higher elevations. Very cold wind chills develop, and\nwill be worst Friday and Saturday nights, where parts of the \nmountains will drop into the 12-20F degree range for the \"feels \nlike\" temperature. While it will be very unpleasant and a shock \nto the system, we will be several degrees too warm for any Cold \nWeather advisories. Temperatures rebound some early next week. \n\nLight rain and eventually snow arrives in the mountains \nThursday through Saturday night. Today looks like the last warm \nday in the freezable future. Spokes of upper level energy \nmoving around an upper low will help trigger surface\ncyclogenesisover the OH and TN Valleys and steer a surface \nsystem into the southern and central Appalachians Thursday. This\nwill trigger light rain showers over the mountains and possibly\ninto the Piedmont. Precipitation will be very light and mix \nwith and eventually change over to light snow as we transition \ninto a NW upslope event Thursday night continuing into Saturday \nmorning, mainly impacting the Greenbrier Valley. Amounts will be\nlow each day and it will be difficult for the snow to stick to \nthe warmer ground initially. But as the event continues we could\nsee some light accumulations. High pressure pushes into the \nregion by Sunday, and the remainder of the weekend will be dry, \nbut cold.",
